WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.768
-Our first guest tonight is
an Academy Award-winning actor

00:00:02.768 --> 00:00:04.837
you know from films such as
"Mystic River",

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:06.806
"Milk", and
"Fast Times at Ridgemont High".

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:08.541
He's the director and star of
"Flag Day",

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:10.043
which is in theaters everywhere
now.

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:12.478
Please welcome to the show
Sean Penn.

00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:17.216 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:17.216 --> 00:00:20.052
Thank you so much
for being here, my friend.

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:23.423 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:25.157
Thank you so much
for being here in person.

00:00:25.157 --> 00:00:26.258
-Thanks for having me.

00:00:26.258 --> 00:00:29.128
-This is based on a book
by Jennifer Vogel

00:00:29.128 --> 00:00:31.030
about her father,
called "Flim-Flam Man",

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:34.167
and it as it crossed your path
years ago.

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:37.570
-Yeah, I read it, oh,
almost 15 years ago,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.072
I think,
and it had a long journey

00:00:40.072 --> 00:00:41.507
getting to the point
of making it,

00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:44.009
and now we've finally
gotten the chance to do that.

00:00:44.009 --> 00:00:46.379
-And this is --
like I mentioned,

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.514
this is about her relationship
with her father,

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:52.551
and you in turn, then decided to
cast your daughter...

00:00:52.551 --> 00:00:54.453
-Mm-hmmm.
-...to play Jennifer.

00:00:54.453 --> 00:00:56.322
Is that something that
occurred to you right away,

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:57.657
as well,
when you were reading it?

00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:59.993
-Yeah, in a very unique way.
With this script,

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:03.529
it -- I was at about page 30

00:01:03.529 --> 00:01:07.033
and her face
just got imprinted on it

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.701
and I couldn't get it
out of my head.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:11.971
And then it was a question
of waiting

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.373
until she was ready to do it.

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:15.041
She didn't want to do it.
She didn't --

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:18.477
She just thought that acting was
the silliest thing in the world

00:01:18.477 --> 00:01:20.980
as a profession, as a teenager.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.784
And as she came
to embrace it more,

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:27.186
I think, principally, as
an avenue to be a director,

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:30.290
which is what she wants to do.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.393
She came into it

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:35.561
and is just extraordinary
in the movie.

00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:36.862
-She's really wonderful,

00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:40.033
and, you know, she plays someone
who is very vulnerable

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.068
because of the way
her father is.

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:44.170
And I imagine, one --
first of all,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.005
acting's very vulnerable
to begin with,

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:47.674
but then as your --
if you're a parent,

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:50.376
you must always feel like your
children seem vulnerable to you.

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:52.878
How -- Was it even harder
to direct her

00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:54.413
because she was your daughter,
especially in those

00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:56.849
tricky father/daughter scenes?
Or was it just natural

00:01:56.849 --> 00:01:59.619
because that is the relationship
you guys have?

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:03.189
-I think that there was a real
shorthand that you gain

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:06.659
from being close for 30 years.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:12.664
And so, somebody calls it
blood harmony,

00:02:12.664 --> 00:02:17.036
then -- there's -- when
it happens, it can be magic.

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:24.477
And watching her, she had always
been so uncontrived as a person.

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:27.513
And to see her as an actress
be so uncontrived

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:30.916
and to be able to watch
her think without being told

00:02:30.916 --> 00:02:35.021
what she's thinking by her
is a very moving thing.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:39.392
So I think that
it was probably helpful.

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:41.460
-Your son, Hopper,
in the film as well.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.896
-Yeah.
-Is this just a father

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:47.466
using jobs in movies to spend
more time with your kids?

00:02:47.466 --> 00:02:48.534
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.436
[ Laughter ]

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:51.837
And did it work? Was it good?

00:02:51.837 --> 00:02:54.340
Was it good
family time together?

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.476
-It was great.
I don't sleep very well,

00:02:56.476 --> 00:02:58.278
but when my kids
are under the same roof

00:02:58.278 --> 00:03:00.413
or in some proximity,
I suddenly sleep.

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:02.215
So I was able to sleep
on this shoot in ways

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:05.318
I hadn't been before.
Yeah, it was --

00:03:05.318 --> 00:03:08.488
And then I've just been -- kind
of took the show on the road

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:10.856
with Dylan, you know,
doing press for the movie.

00:03:10.856 --> 00:03:14.360
And so she still gets
ball and chained to me.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.394
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:15.394 --> 00:03:17.029
-It's a good plan.

00:03:17.029 --> 00:03:20.032
-Did you have -- What was
Jennifer Vogel's relationship

00:03:20.032 --> 00:03:21.700
with the project?
Did you talk to her a lot?

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.971
I mean, obviously, this is
so intensely personal to her.

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:28.641
-Yeah. I mean, we just lucked
out that Jennifer Vogel

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:32.912
happens to be the most
extraordinarily supportive,

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:35.281
even of poetic license.

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:37.583
You know, she gets it.
She gets film.

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:40.086
So she was really
a collaborator.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.655
And there were times where
we were making revisions --

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:48.093
we'd always bounce it by her.
She came to set a few times.

00:03:48.093 --> 00:03:51.864
You know, her book is the movie
and then some,

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:54.333
because we I just tried to
express

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:57.403
the center core
of the story with the movie.

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:59.905
But she has -- I mean, the book,
"Flim-Flam Man"

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:02.341
is an extraordinarily
well-written book.

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:04.710
-After this really
difficult 18 months,

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.012
some movies are lucky enough
to be in theaters again.

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:09.848
I know you appreciate
watching a movie in theaters.

00:04:09.848 --> 00:04:13.519
Is it extra special that this is
a film that can be consumed

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.354
that way as well as at home?

00:04:15.354 --> 00:04:18.591
-Yeah, for me, the fact that
it's in the theaters

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:21.126
and particularly that
it's exclusively

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:25.765
in the theaters, because I think
that baits those like me.

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:29.335
You know, if I'm interested
in seeing a film,

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:33.239
I don't want the convenience
of the small box

00:04:33.239 --> 00:04:35.174
when something was,
as "Flag Day" was,

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:37.176
made for the big screen.

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:40.313
And so I was very fortunate,
you know, in this day and age

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:45.084
that I have an MGM distributor
who believed in it that way

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:47.720
and gave us
this exclusive period.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.955
Of course,
it will eventually stream.

00:04:49.955 --> 00:04:53.125
But I do always feel,
at this point in time,

00:04:53.125 --> 00:04:57.530
that I have to say that
I hope -- I'd ask

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:01.600
that as as much as I want people
all to go to the theater,

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.537
I really only want people
who are vaccinated and safe

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:06.872
to themselves
and each other to go.

00:05:06.872 --> 00:05:08.140
And it will stream,

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:11.010
but most of the people
who aren't vaccinated

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.912
probably aren't interested
in my movies anymore.

00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:14.913
-Yeah, that's a good point.
[ Laughs ]

00:05:14.913 --> 00:05:18.484
Did you -- Do you like watching
yourself on a big screen

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:20.486
or do you have
the anxiety of somebody

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:22.188
just watching themselves
perform?

00:05:22.188 --> 00:05:23.956
-I am not my own aesthetic.

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:25.557
-OK, got you.
-You know, as a director --

00:05:25.557 --> 00:05:27.460
It's why I've never
cast myself before.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:28.494
-[ Laughs ] Oh, that's true.

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.028
So you've directed
multiple films.

00:05:30.028 --> 00:05:31.664
This is the first time you're
directing yourself as an actor.

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:33.332
Yeah.
Were you difficult?

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:34.634
Did you find yourself
hard to work with?

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:39.005
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, no,
I...

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:43.075
I found one thing really made --
a surprising thing,

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:45.645
because, you know,
it's a lot more work

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:49.815
and it's already a 17 1/2 hour
a day job to direct a movie.

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:50.983
But I didn't have to --

00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:52.918
you know, with the principal
two actors,

00:05:52.918 --> 00:05:56.422
which is led by Dylan,
but me,

00:05:56.422 --> 00:05:59.725
I didn't have to negotiate
the ego politics

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:01.928
that you do.
You know, you've given an actor

00:06:01.928 --> 00:06:03.796
10 takes on this side
and you come around

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:05.097
on the other, you give him two
and they say,

00:06:05.097 --> 00:06:06.765
"What's this about?"

00:06:06.765 --> 00:06:10.336
And it would have been four,
because you would have done

00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:12.171
an extra two takes
to make them feel good,

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:15.173
but your focus is on Jennifer,
who's the lead in the story,

00:06:15.173 --> 00:06:17.743
and you really want to put
your time in there.

00:06:17.743 --> 00:06:19.512
So, it was easy.

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:23.182
That was one thing that offered
a little less fatigue.

00:06:23.182 --> 00:06:26.018
-That's nice.
I want to come back, ask more,

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:27.152
and we're going to show
a clip as well.

00:06:27.152 --> 00:06:28.888
Be right back with more
from Sean Penn.

